# Who must review what.
#
# This repository had no CODEOWNERS and its own August architecture
# assessment measured the reason it needed one: roughly 99.6% of commits
# were agent-authored, with no independent review to reject the 4,000-line
# functions as they grew. That is not an argument about who writes code. An
# agent writing and another invocation of the same agent reviewing share
# their assumptions, so the second pass cannot find what the first one could
# not see.
#
# The paths below are the ones where a wrong assumption is expensive: the
# security boundary, the runtime foundation, persistent state, the release
# and dependency contract, and the gates themselves. tools/check_review_policy.py
# fails when a path listed here stops existing, so this file cannot rot into
# a list of directories that were renamed years ago.
#
# Ordering follows CODEOWNERS semantics: the LAST matching pattern wins, so
# the general rule comes first and the specific ones after it.

# Default owner for everything not named below.
*                               @youngbryan97

